<Placemark>   <name>'Ahu'ena Heiau</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <b>County/Region:</b> The West <br /> Pos Accuracy: <b>4</b> Condition: <b>3</b> (5 is best)   Ambience: <b>4</b>  Access: <b>4</b>  (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 19.6389 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: -155.997547<img src="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/a558/a312/gallery/The_Americas/USA/The_West/thumb/flk39512579260.jpg" align="right" valign="top"></a>
<br />
                    This is a temple complex that was rededicated by Kamehameha I in 1812 and served as the seat of his government until his death in 1819.  However, the rectangular stone base of the Heiau predates Kameh...<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
		     <a href="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=30794" >Link To More Information</a><br />
                    ]]></description>    <styleUrl>#64_r</styleUrl>   <Point>     <coordinates>-155.997547,19.6389,0</coordinates>   </Point>  </Placemark>
<Placemark>   <name>Abu Erteila</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <br /> Pos Accuracy: <b>3</b> Condition: <b>1</b> (5 is best)   (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 16.86 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: 33.75<br />
                    Ancient Temple in Republic of the Sudan.<br/>All that remains of this Meroitic Period (fourth century BC) temple complex are are six foot high koms (mounds.)  Long buried in the sediments of the Wadi ...<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
		     <a href="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=35432" >Link To More Information</a><br />
                    ]]></description>    <styleUrl>#64_g</styleUrl>   <Point>     <coordinates>33.75,16.86,0</coordinates>   </Point>  </Placemark>
<Placemark>   <name>Abu Simbel Temple of Nefertari</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <b>County/Region:</b> Nubia <br />Condition: <b>5</b> (5 is best)   Ambience: <b>5</b>  Access: <b>4</b>  (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 22.33756 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: 31.62681<img src="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/a558/a312/gallery/tunisia/Tunisia_and_North_Africa/nubia/thumb/Abu_Simbel_Temple_of_Nefertari_01.JPG" align="right" valign="top"></a>
<br />
                    Temple in Nubia. <br />Nefertari was Ramses II favourite wife. This temple stands about 100 meters north of Ramses' own much larger temple.There are four colossi of Ramses and only two of Nefertari in...<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
		     <a href="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=6336047" >Link To More Information</a><br />
                    ]]></description>    <styleUrl>#64_r</styleUrl>   <Point>     <coordinates>31.62681,22.33756,0</coordinates>   </Point>  </Placemark>
<Placemark>   <name>Abu Simbel Temple of Ramses II</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <b>County/Region:</b> Nubia <br />Condition: <b>5</b> (5 is best)   Ambience: <b>5</b>  Access: <b>4</b>  (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 22.33689 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: 31.6256<img src="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/a558/a312/gallery/tunisia/Tunisia_and_North_Africa/nubia/thumb/Abu_Simbel_Temple_of_Ramses_01.JPG" align="right" valign="top"></a>
<br />
                    Temple in Nubia. <br />All the Egyptian temples in Nubia were saved from flooding during the building of the Aswan High Dam by a concerted effort of Unesco and participating countries such as Italy, S...<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
		     <a href="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=6336048" >Link To More Information</a><br />
                    ]]></description>    <styleUrl>#64_r</styleUrl>   <Point>     <coordinates>31.6256,22.33689,0</coordinates>   </Point>  </Placemark>
<Placemark>   <name>Abydos Temple of Ramses II</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <b>County/Region:</b> Upper Egypt (South) <br /> Pos Accuracy: <b>5</b> Condition: <b>3</b> (5 is best)   Ambience: <b>4</b>  Access: <b>5</b>  (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 26.186458 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: 31.916319<br />
                    Temple in Upper Egypt. 18th Dynasty temple of Ramses II. The roof is missing, but the releifs and colours remain in good condition....<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
		     <a href="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=6336050" >Link To More Information</a><br />
                    ]]></description>    <styleUrl>#64_g</styleUrl>   <Point>     <coordinates>31.916319,26.186458,0</coordinates>   </Point>  </Placemark>
<Placemark>   <name>Abydos Temple of Seti I</name>   <description><![CDATA[
        <b>Type:</b> Ancient Temple <b>County/Region:</b> Upper Egypt (South) <b>Alt Name:</b> Abdju Temple of Seti I <br /> Pos Accuracy: <b>4</b> Condition: <b>4</b> (5 is best)   Ambience: <b>5</b>  Access: <b>5</b>  (5 is best)<br />
                    <b>Lat:</b> 26.18548 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Long</b>: 31.9196<img src="http://www.megalithic.co.uk/a558/a312/gallery/tunisia/Tunisia_and_North_Africa/upper_egypt/thumb/Abydos_Osiris_and_Seti_I.JPG" align="right" valign="top"></a>
<br />
                    Temple in Upper Egypt.  Exquisite 19th Dynasty temple built by Seti I and dedicated to seven gods, in particular the triad of Osiris, Isis and Horus.  Ptah and Amun are also venerated.  The temple has...<b>.</b> (c) Meg. Portal contributors.<br />
